\chapter{Introduction}
  \label{chap:intro}
  The idea of Louisiana in the public imagination is most often rather that of South Louisiana and the New Orleans metropolitan area as shown in Figure \ref{fig:mapacadiana} where the highlighted area is of the traditional French speaking parishes (\foreign{i.e.}, counties) of the state.
  Indeed, the boundary between North and South Louisiana is traditionally a division in culinary practices, religion, and language \parencite[p.~309]{trepanier_french_1988}, where those cultural features that most distinguish Louisiana from the rest of the Deep South are those of South Louisiana.
  The language of North Louisiana, for example, has historically been English rather than French and Creole \parencite[p.~265]{gold_french_1979}, and the most prominent religion has historically been Protestant Christianity rather than Catholicism.
  At times, this difference in religious beliefs has even been significant enough that North Louisianians have been described as ``rabid anti-Catholic Protestants'' \parencite[p.~27]{johnson_louisiana_1976}.
  This has led to a situation where South Louisiana locals will even sometimes derisively refer to North Louisiana as ``South Arkansas'', a reference to the perceived indistinguishability of North Louisiana from the rest of the mostly anglophone Protestant American South.

  \begin{figure}[tbhp]
    \centering
    <<>>=
    mapacadianacities
    @
    \caption{Acadiana according to HCR 496 \parencite{trepanier_french_1988}}
    \label{fig:mapacadiana}
  \end{figure}

  For sociolinguists, the two major social categories of South Louisiana, Creole and Cajun, constitute a major point of interest.
  Both of these categories may be conceptualized as ethnic categories in the sense that they are flexibly defined through group negotiation of who is and who is not a member (\citeauthor{brubaker_ethnicity_2002}, \citeyear{brubaker_ethnicity_2002}; \citeauthor{giles_ethnicity_1979}, \citeyear[pp.~252-253]{giles_ethnicity_1979}; \citeauthor{le_page_acts_1985}, \citeyear[p.~2]{le_page_acts_1985}; \citeauthor{moerman_ethnic_1965}, \citeyear{moerman_ethnic_1965}), but as will be seen through the winding histories of the definitions of the two terms, to be discussed in Sections \ref{sec:creole_history} and \ref{sec:cajun_history}, they are also entwined with racialized social categories \parencite{omi_racial_2015} from the broader US.\footnote{
    All of these category types are of course social constructs.
  }
  In general, the term \lexi{Creole} has referred to various groups based on geographical origin, status as free or enslaved, or skin color, ending up as something of a synonym for Black for many people today, although the modern-day use of the term certainly maintains some variability.
  The term \lexi{Cajun}, on the other hand, started as a term referring to the people of the Acadian diaspora in Louisiana and has morphed into something that refers to people from various backgrounds with the notable restriction that Cajun and Black identities are essentially mutually exclusive.
  Much of the history of the changing definitions of these categories occurred in the context of Louisiana as a French and Spanish colony before becoming part of the United States, but the state's gradual rapprochement with the US has also led to influence from the racialized social system \parencite{bonilla-silva_rethinking_1997} of the broader country.
  
  Of more linguistic interest, language is a distinguishing feature of South Louisiana in that there is a history of Creoles and Cajuns speaking French and Creole (F\&C)\footnote{
    \lexi{F\&C} will be used as an umbrella term in this study for all French-related language varieties spoken in South Louisiana, as will be discussed further in Section \ref{sec:french_vs_creole}.
  }.
  Today, these languages are spoken in South Louisiana as something akin to heritage languages, whereas they were the dominant languages perhaps as recently as the mid 20th century.
  By the 1980s in fact, South Louisiana was still being described as ``un exemple par excellence d'une société multilingue''\footnote{
    an ideal example of a multilingual society
  } \parencite[p.~16]{neumann-holzschuh_creole_1985}.
  F\&C in Louisiana is a unique cultural resource that is generally valued by many among the populace and even exploited for commercial gain, and yet it is in decline.
  It has been documented as showing variation along social dimensions, as will be discussed in Section \ref{sec:pronoun_structure}, although recent work focusing on variation along the lines of ethnic and/or racialized identities in and around the Lafayette area, the geographic focus of this study, is lacking.
  With the hope of filling this gap in the descriptive literature on F\&C, at least partially, while also elucidating some portion of the social worlds of Creoles and Cajuns, the objective of this dissertation is to examine the subject pronoun systems of both groups and how these relate to speakers' identities.
  Chapter \ref{chap:pronoun_variation} will therefore give a more traditional variationist analysis of subject pronoun usage in order to establish the subject pronoun tendencies of Creoles and Cajuns generally, asking if F\&C subject pronouns vary systematically between the two groups.
  Chapter \ref{chap:networks} will look at the personal social networks of participants to examine ethnic homophily in those networks and if this more fine-grained social measure can explain, in particular, speakers who do not fit the general subject pronoun patterns.
  Finally, Chapter \ref{chap:discourse} will examine how participants speak about their identities and language with a special focus on how subject pronouns can be exploited in social meaning-making and the construction of Creole and Cajun identities, providing context for understanding the quantitative results of the previous chapters.

  \section{Race and ethnicity in sociolinguistics}
    \label{sec:race_sociolinguistics}
    Both race and ethnicity are implicated when examining Creoles and Cajuns given the contestations around how they are defined in the community today and the outsized role of the broader US racialized social system in those contestations.
    In sociolinguistics and language variation studies, race and ethnicity have been analyzed as far back as the foundational works in the field.
    \textcite{labov_social_2006} examined Italians, Jewish people \parencite{labov_social_2006, labov_contraction_1969, labov_sociolinguistic_1972}, and African Americans in New York City while \textcite{wolfram_sociolinguistic_1969} looked at African Americans in Detroit, for instance.
    The field progressed in its treatment of these categories in much the same way as the field progressed in general, from the macro-sociological level to the ethnographic and local level to social meaning-oriented work, referred to as the three waves of sociolinguistics \parencite{eckert_three_2012}, but these specific categories have had their own idiosyncrasies in how they have been approached over time.

    Much of the research that came out of the first wave of sociolinguistics was aimed at showing that the languages used by marginalized groups are logical and rule-bound.
    This does not mean that researchers did not touch on connections to social issues, but social issues were not yet the focus of this work.
    For instance, \textcite{labov_social_2006} analyzed Italians, Jewish people, and African Americans in New York City and found that they all had their own vowel usage patterns in English.
    Notably, for some of the ongoing vowel changes in New York City, African American usage ``follow[ed] an entirely different pattern'' \parencite[p.~247]{labov_social_2006}, which one might interpret as segregation between Black and White Americans on some level that was finding expression in their speech.
    Although this was not a point Labov seemed to be attempting to make, it was nonetheless an important social finding present in that early work.

    While first wave studies of language variation went a long way towards reducing the stigma attached to the language varieties of marginalized people, the framing was not always ideal.
    One framing that arguably undermined researchers' laudable goals was the regular use of the qualifier \lexi{non-standard} for varieties spoken mostly by minority groups \parencite[\foreign{e.g.},][p.~715]{labov_contraction_1969}, but perhaps the most significant framing issue was the idea that African American Language (AAL) \parencite[pp.~286-289]{king_african_2020} was a single monolithic variety, which may have simply been the good intention of wanting to demonstrate the logical structure of AAL being taken too far.
    Fieldwork on AAL from locations as varied as Philadelphia, Chicago, Detroit, Cleveland, and Los Angeles were all described as essentially the same language variety with only a few minor variations \parencite[p.~715]{labov_contraction_1969}.
    Even by the 1990s, well into the second wave of sociolinguistics, African American speech was still being described, for example, as ``the uniform grammar used by African Americans who have minimal contact with other dialects typically in contexts where only speakers of that vernacular are present'' \parencite[p.~125]{labov_co-existent_2022}\footnote{
      Labov actually attributed this definition to \textcite{baugh_black_1983}, but the latter did not provide a definition.
    } or as a continuum, called African American English in this case, ranging from essentially the privileged varieties in American English to African American Vernacular English \parencite[p.~xxi]{rickford_african_1999}.
    Such aggregating of the speech of disparate groups of people risked being a source of erasure \parencite{irvine_language_2000} not only of distinct ways of speaking but of the distinct identities that are attached to those ways of speaking.

    The second wave of sociolinguistics was said to have begun with \textcite{milroy_language_1987} \parencite[p.~91]{eckert_three_2012}.
    The attention that AAL received during the first wave diminished during this time, and what work was being done in that area involved more fine-grained analyses.
    For example, \textcite{rickford_addressee-_1994} examined constraints on style shifting relative to the racialized identities of an African American woman's interlocutors.
    In zooming in to more local contexts, more local social categories also became the focus.
    In one case tangentially related to the current study, the verbal morphology of ``Cajun Vernacular English'' was analyzed not only on its own but in relation to White southern varieties as well as AAL, suggesting a complex relationship between Cajuns and these other groups.
    However, while local categories such as Cajun were being considered, they were not receiving the sort of close critical treatment that they merited.
    Cajun identity itself was thus not defined at all by \textcite{dubois_verbal_2003} but instead implied to simply be the identity of White people in South Louisiana.

    The usefulness of social categories such as ethnicity was examined during the second wave, as well.
    \textcite{horvath_delimiting_1987}, although not working on language in the US, explicitly discussed the utility of ethnicity as a social variable.
    They argued that ethnicity is not well defined because it might have varying degrees of influence depending on which immigration generation one is in as well as between those in the same immigration generation but from different communities \parencite[p.~183]{horvath_delimiting_1987}.
    In the latter point, they were avoiding the traps into which earlier researchers fell when establishing AAL as a monolith.
    They were also making immigration the central factor in ethnic group formation, similar to what \textcite{labov_social_2006} had implicitly done by singling out Italians in New York City.
    This aligned with the emphasis that some sociologists have placed on immigration for the formation of ethnic identities at that time \parencite[as cited in][p.~903]{bonilla-silva_essential_1999}.
    Indeed, scholars as diverse as \textcite{weber_economy_1978} and \textcite{eriksen_ethnicity_2010} have highlighted contact as a necessary condition for ethnic identities to form.
    Contact has likewise been central to the theory of enregisterment from the third wave of sociolinguistics for named language varieties \parencite{agha_social_2003, johnstone_mobility_2006}.
    The point being that, by the end of the second wave, researchers had begun to be more cognizant of the need to reconsider how social variables are used and understood, and that included ethnicity even if race was not being prominently addressed at that time.

    The third wave of sociolinguistics turned the focus of research towards social meaning and local categories even more so, making it perhaps not surprising that race has been examined more carefully during this phase.
    One area to which this care has been extended is in studies of crossing (\foreign{i.e.}, using out-group features) \parencite{bucholtz_you_1999, eberhardt_first_2015, reyes_appropriation_2005}.
    Intersectional analyses of in-group variation have also been more prominent \parencite{eckert_where_2008, mendoza-denton_homegirls:_2008}.
    An example for AAL is incorporating socioeconomic class when examining African American speakers \parencite{bucholtz_sociolinguistic_2003, rahman_middle-class_2008} rather than focusing almost entirely on a very specific subset of African Americans as past studies had done, namely poor, young, urban men.
    For AAL, this more intersectional approach carries over into undoing the monolithic framing inherited from previous eras of sociolinguistic research where regional variation is now acknowledged and efforts to map that variation have been undertaken (\citeauthor{holliday_multiracial_2019}, \citeyear{holliday_multiracial_2019}; \citeauthor{jones_toward_2015}, \citeyear{jones_toward_2015}; \citeauthor{king_forging_2019}, \citeyear{king_forging_2019}; \citeauthor{wolfram_american_2016}, \citeyear[p.~232]{wolfram_american_2016}).

    More recently, raciolinguistics \parencite{rosa_unsettling_2017} has become a prominent paradigm in sociolinguistics, which is informed by sociological concepts such as racial formation/racialization \parencite{omi_racial_2015} and racialized social systems \parencite{bonilla-silva_rethinking_1997}, two concepts that likewise inform how race is understood in the present study.
    Central to the raciolinguistics approach is examining how society naturalizes (\foreign{i.e.}, makes something seem natural) racialized groups, racialized languages, and the connections between them as this activity is seen as a ``central component[] of the European colonial formation of modernity'' \parencite[p.~623]{rosa_unsettling_2017}.
    In practice, much of this research has examined not just language ideologies but raciolinguistic ideologies, which in part builds on more general sociolinguistic work on enregisterment \parencite{agha_social_2003}.
    Raciolinguistic ideologies are conceptualized as bi-directional in the sense that language ideologies inform racial ideologies and \foreign{vice versa}.
    In effect, one can ``look like a language and sound like a race'' \parencite{rosa_looking_2019}.
    Applying ideological work in a more variationist vein, \textcite{king_african_2020} argued for centering ethnographic context into raciolinguistic analyses in order to be less dogmatic in who is included or whose speech is included when studying race.
    This involves focusing more on how race is socially constructed through the use of linguistic features and how those linguistic features in turn socially construct race.
    For example, African Americans of different generations and different place orientations in Rochester, NY were seen as navigating the \vowel{bat} vowel, involved in the Northern Cities Vowel Shift, differently from each other to construct their various identities, and those navigations in turn played into the larger negotiations of the indexicalities that this vowel had for the community as a whole \parencite{king_rethinking_2021}.
    Likewise, in Bakersfield, CA, African American men were seen constructing their sociolinguistic identities by matching the /s/ fronting used by both Black and White women in order to avoid the indexical link between non-fronting as used by White country men \parencite{calder_whose_2022}.
    In other words, race was playing a role in how African American men spoke there, which in turn played a role in how the local African American man persona was constructed.
    Not limited to African Americans or even the US, however, the raciolinguistic perspective has been applied to understanding how the raciolinguistic ideologies of the inspectorate impact education \parencite{cushing_white_2023} and how English language raciolinguistic ideologies in higher education shape the experiences of international Chinese students \parencite{gao_language_2025}, both analyses being situated in the UK.
    This research track has born out significant advances in how race and ethnicity are understood sociolinguistically.

    Race and ethnicity have, in sum, always held a prominent place in sociolinguistics despite having been dealt with differently over time.
    Early work did much to establish AAL and other language varieties associated with marginalized groups as rule-bound and logical.
    Second and early third wave studies began looking more closely at local categories while giving more fine-grained analyses of broader social categories.
    It was also at that time that the validity of social categories was questioned.
    More recently, sociological work on race has informed how race is used in sociolinguistics today through the raciolinguistics perspective.

  \subsection{Race and ethnicity in the United States}
    \label{sec:united_states}
    While the specific social milieu that will be analyzed here is situated in mostly rural South Louisiana, it would be difficult to interpret the local categories of Creole and Cajun without considering their place in the broader context of the country as a whole.
    The most important aspect of that broader context is perhaps the racialized social system of the US given how pervasive it is and its consequent influence on how local social categories are understood.
    Despite South Louisiana historically being considered isolated from the United States, the undoing of that isolation over time has in fact been a factor not only in the decline of F\&C in the state but also in the reinterpretation of Creole and Cajun identities, as will be discussed in Sections \ref{sec:creole_history} and \ref{sec:cajun_history}.
    For the present section, the discussion will roughly follow \citeauthor{marable_great_2002}'s (\citeyear{marable_great_2002}) outlining of the three ``racial domains'' through which African Americans have passed: slavery, Jim Crow, and finally modern ghettos.
    This is certainly not say that African Americans (as well as Black people in general) are the only groups that have been racialized in the US, but they hold a prominent position both in public discourse dealing with racialized groups as well as in studies of racialization and so their plight over time is useful for contextualizing how race and ethnicity function in the US.
    Additionally, as will be seen later, there are clear connections between the categories Creole, Black, and African American for Louisianians.

    The European colonial enterprise in the Americas began with the earnest exploitation of Others.
    This culminated most prominently with the enslavement of Africans who were then trafficked into the colonies to perform labor for European colonists.
    The racialization of Africans (\foreign{i.e.}, their dehumanization) undoubtedly came about to justify this gross human rights abuse.
    In total, it has been estimated that 9,778,500 people were enslaved and trafficked into the Americas between 1450 and 1867, whereas an estimated 11,698,000 were deported from Africa between 1450 and 1900 \parencite[p.~477]{lovejoy_volume_1982}.
    This implies that upwards of 1,919,500 lives were lost just in the process of moving people alone.
    For the British colonies, these people made up a larger proportion of the southern population (39.8\%) than the middle (7.0\%) or the northern (3.1\%) populations \parencite[p.~74]{franklin_slavery_2000}.
    The territory of modern-day Louisiana, of course was not part of the British colonies but rather part of New France, Spain after the 1760s, and ultimately the US after the Louisiana Purchase of 1803.
    Between 1763 and 1800, Lower Louisiana was consistently divided almost evenly between free people and enslaved people \parencite[p.~279]{hall_africans_1992}.
    This is to say that interactions between colonists, people of indigenous tribes, and enslaved people in the colonies in the Americas involved significant social stratification, especially in what would become the state of Louisiana.

    After the abolition of slavery, by which time Louisiana was well established as a US state, the Reconstruction era gave rise to debates over how to integrate formerly enslaved people into society, perhaps the most important of which was between W.E.B. Du Bois and Booker T. Washington \parencite{morris_scholar_2015}.
    By design, limits placed on Black people in the US at the time mostly prevented them from moving too far up the social ladder towards full inclusion into society, hence even those who improved their lot often hit a ceiling that resulted, it has been argued, in three racialized social classes: working-class Black people, a small Black bourgeoisie, and White people \parencite{allen_black_1990}.
    The Black bourgeoisie faced pressure from White people to keep those in the lower socioeconomic class under control, which helped the Black bourgeoisie keep their own standing while still perpetuating the hierarchy of racialized categories, a pattern that some Black college students have felt even into recent years \parencite[p.~431]{holliday_sociolinguistic_2021}.
    This is perhaps a common feature in racialized social systems as similar middle status groups with similar roles developed in Brazil, referred to as Pardos \parencite[pp.~74-77]{wade_race_2010}, as well as in South Africa, referred to as Coloureds there \parencite{lewis_between_1987}.
    Creoles in the Americas in general have also been described in similar terms \parencite[pp.~59-60]{anderson_imagined_2006}, including those in Louisiana, as will be discussed in Section \ref{sec:creole_history}.

    A theme of oppression is that it often becomes entrenched in such a way that it can appear to be diminishing when in reality it is simply changing in appearance.
    A particularly vivid example of this change in appearance is the Jim Crow era in the US, which presented indications of improvement that either did not last, were not truly significant, or were simply new chains in disguise.
    While Black people had gained assets by the 1920s, for instance, their totals made up an extremely small proportion of the total assets held in the country and in some cases, such as the Tulsa Race Massacre of 1921, were violently destroyed (\citeauthor{albright_after_2021}, \citeyear[p.~1]{albright_after_2021}; \citeauthor{harris_negro_1936}, \citeyear[pp.~194-195]{harris_negro_1936}; \citeauthor{watkins_money_nodate}, \citeyear{watkins_money_nodate}), and while Historically Black Colleges and Universities (HBCUs) had developed, their boards were often undermined by White interests \parencite[p.~559]{williamson_this_2004}.
    Additionally, even though racial segregation existed already after the Civil War, it was relatively minor compared to the extreme racial segregation that had developed across the country by the 1940s, including in Louisiana (\citeauthor{berlin_slaves_1974}, \citeyear[pp.~257-258]{berlin_slaves_1974}; \citeauthor{taeurber_negroes_1965}, \citeyear[pp.~39-41]{taeurber_negroes_1965}).
    The Jim Crow laws that engendered this segregation legally ended with the Civil Rights Act of 1964 and both \court{Jones v. Mayer} and the Fair Housing Act in 1968 \parencite[pp.~viii-ix]{rothstein_color_2017}, but if this could be seen as the end of a colonial administration, it was certainly not the end of the colonial situation \parencite[pp.~325-326]{grosfoguel_race_2004} nor of \foreign{de facto} segregation.
    The racialized social system of the US simply changed its appearance once more while continuing to create unequal life chances for marginalized groups up to the present despite increasing diversity and a reduction in overt acts of intolerance.

    Census records for the United States suggest that diversity has indeed increased, shifting from 1 in 8 people being non-White to 1 in 4.
    When including Latinxs, the ratio becomes 1 in 3 \parencite[pp.~25-26]{fischer_century_2006}.
    This pattern holds through the 2020s with the difference that non-White people increased to 42\% of the population when including Latinxs.
    Latinxs have in fact become the largest non-White group at 19.5\% of the population, followed by Black people at 13.7\% \parencite{us_census_bureau_us_2020-1}.
    At the level of the neighborhood, census records suggest that the proportion of majority White neighborhoods in the country decreased from 66.0\% to 42.5\% between 1990 and 2010, which may be driven by immigrants mostly settling in highly populous metropolitan areas \parencite[pp.~175-176]{wright_patterns_2014}.
    Despite this increased diversity, segregation has persisted in occupational domains, education, and housing both nationally and in Louisiana (\citeauthor{egalite_impact_2016}, \citeyear{egalite_impact_2016}; \citeauthor{mizutani_backyard_2019}, \citeyear{mizutani_backyard_2019}; \citeauthor{rothstein_color_2017}, \citeyear[pp.~xiii-xiv]{rothstein_color_2017}; \citeauthor{scott_place_2023}, \citeyear{scott_place_2023}; \citeauthor{tomaskovic-devey_documenting_2006}, \citeyear{tomaskovic-devey_documenting_2006}; \citeauthor{wright_patterns_2014}, \citeyear[pp.~176-177]{wright_patterns_2014}).
    White Americans have, however, taken notice of the increased diversity even with the persistence of segregation.
    Already by 2000, 20\% of Americans thought that White people formed a minority of the population \parencite[p.~905]{alba_distorted_2005}.
    This increased diversity coupled with the belief that White people represent a minority group may have led to a backlash against efforts to solve systemic inequalities.
    As \textcite{omi_racial_1997} observed among White college students in the late 1990s, they questioned their own identities, in apparent resentment, in response to seeing programs and initiatives in place meant to help non-White groups (p.~20), signaling that their Whiteness had become less invisible, so to speak \parencite{lewis_what_2004}, than it once was.

    Although in general the US has experienced improved relations in terms of outwardly expressed tolerance of minority racialized groups between the 1970s and the 2010s \parencite{bobo_real_2012, firebaugh_trends_1988}, there are still signs of persistent systemic problems.
    Income inequality between such groups, for one, had been decreasing in the 20th century but stalled by 2000 (\citeauthor{cotter_systems_1999}, \citeyear{cotter_systems_1999}; \citeauthor{van_hofwegen_coming_2010}, \citeyear[p.~431]{van_hofwegen_coming_2010}).
    Systemic issues become readily apparent in cases where both Black and White Americans should face similar hurdles to good life outcomes but do not.
    In cities like Milwaukee and New York, for example, it was found that not only did Black people with criminal records have more difficulty obtaining employment than White people with criminal records, but Black people in general had more difficulty in life than White people with criminal records \parencite{pager_mark_2003, pager_discrimination_2009}.
    Pronouncements of ideological racism may not be prevalent in the US anymore, but this has not led to the deracialization of the social system.

    Ideological racism, in fact, does not need to be pronounced in a society where Whiteness is treated as unmarked as in the US.
    \citeauthor{omi_racial_1997}'s (\citeyear{omi_racial_1997}) observation of White college students indeed exemplified that White people in the US have been in a position to not have to concern themselves with their own racialized identity.
    Likewise, Statistical Policy Directive 15 in the US, which sets standards for federal racial and ethnic data, linked Black people to groups in Africa whereas no such reference was made linking White people to European groups nor any other groups \parencite[p.~11]{omi_racial_1997}, implying that once Whiteness is achieved, a group's ancestry no longer matters.
    It is perhaps for this reason that \textcite{waters_ethnic_1990} reported that her White participants saw their ethnicities as ``flexible and symbolic and voluntary'' whereas that was not the case for non-White people (p.~156).
    This unmarked character of Whiteness also carries over into organizations such as evangelical churches \parencite{bracey_white_2016}.
    In this overall situation, the markedness applied to non-White people is itself enough to legitimate the idea that being White is the status that one should strive to obtain.

    In sum, the racialization of the social system of the United States began before the country was even founded.
    Indigenous and enslaved people were exploited from the moment Europeans landed in the Americas, and that exploitation was justified by dehumanizing them as lesser than (White) Europeans.
    While there have been both changes in laws (\foreign{e.g.}, the abolition of slavery, the undoing of Jim Crow laws) and in the acceptability of explicitly expressing ideological racism in public, significant systemic problems have persisted that still result in unequal life outcomes between groups.
    Whiteness remains a privileged status within the racialized social system of the US against which other racialized identities are measured.
    This is the national context in which Creoles and Cajuns find themselves.
  
  \section{The history of Creole people}
    \label{sec:creole_history}
    When focusing in on Louisiana, two social categories that are often evoked are Creole and Cajun.
    The former category and the related group have a rather complex and winding history.
    At the outset of the colonization of Louisiana, a Creole was simply someone born in the colony regardless of any other social characteristics.\footnote{
      It is notable that people of the tribes who resided in the territory have not figured into discussions in the literature about who was and who was not considered a Creole in the early colonial history of Louisiana, although they were quite obviously being born in the territory.
    }
    This was not a particularly distinguishing status until the Louisiana Purchase, at which time the arrival of Americans made being a Creole something more notable than it had been previously.
    After the Civil War and through much of the Jim Crow years, there were efforts to redefine the term as applying to only White people of French or Spanish descent, but this effort was not entirely successful as perhaps the most common understanding of Creole for Louisianians today is as something of a synonym for Black.
    These developments will be discussed in more detail in this section to contextualize the sociohistorical background relevant to the Creole and Cajun participants in this study.

    Before outlining the history of Creoles, one important caveat must be mentioned.
    Much of the research on the various stages in the development of the definition of Creoles has focused on New Orleans rather than the state as a whole.
    However, especially for the 18th century, this is a sensible focus as New Orleans was the colonial population center of the territory.
    Censuses of rural plantations and other settlements in South Louisiana in the 1700s were not numerous but were taken (\citeauthor{mills_family_1981}, \citeyear[p.~27]{mills_family_1981}; \citeauthor{zitomersky_urbanization_1974}, \citeyear[pp.~276-277]{zitomersky_urbanization_1974}).
    These numbers indicate that, for example, there were 4,983 colonists and enslaved people living in the territory of modern day Louisiana -- not including people of indigenous tribes nor women -- of which 1,183, or about 24\%, were outside of New Orleans, mostly in Pointe Coupee \parencite[p.~177]{hall_africans_1992}.
    In contrast, there were 1,005,281 people estimated to reside in the entire New Orleans metropolitan area in 2020 \parencite{us_census_bureau_metropolitan_2020} compared to the 4,657,757 living in the entire state \parencite{us_census_bureau_us_2020}, meaning that roughly 78\% of the population was living outside of New Orleans.
    The region of focus for this study is around the Lafayette area, a good distance from New Orleans, so research specifically on that area rather than on New Orleans would be ideal, but given the disparity in earlier populations, it is still reasonable to assume that early views on Creoles were not significantly different in the rural areas of Louisiana from those in New Orleans.
    As the rural population increased later in the 19th century and beyond, more research also becomes available from rural perspectives.

    The term \lexi{Creole} was originally no more than a synonym for local or native to the colonies of the American continents, a usage that dates at least as far back as 1731 \parencite[pp.~95-97]{dominguez_white_1986}.
    This means not only were French and Spanish colonists considered to be Creoles but so were many enslaved Africans and free people of color, often referred to in the literature using the original French term \foreign{gens de couleur libres}.
    Free people of color were not unique to Louisiana, but they were particularly common there.
    They were also regularly implicated in redefinitions of the term \lexi{Creole}, especially during Reconstruction.
    A court case involving one free person of color, François Tioucou, suggests that they were present in New Orleans since at least the early 18th century.
    Tioucou was not, however, a Creole as he was born in Africa \parencite[pp.~32-33]{everett_free_1966}, reaffirming that \lexi{free person of color} did not necessarily imply Creole at that time.
    
    A common path to freedom for women born in Louisiana and there offspring was through a system known as \foreign{plaçage} in which free men entered into relationships with enslaved women or free women of color that in many ways resembled marriages.
    According to the French Black Code, or \foreign{Code noir}, that was in effect at that time, free men were not permitted to marry nor ``vivre en concubinage''\footnote{
      cohabit
    } with such women \parencite[pp.~3-4]{louis_xv_code_1727}.
    Despite this, there was a strong incentive to have these liaisons anyway.
    Not only did men greatly outnumber women, but both White men and free women of color had much longer life expectancies than White women and free men of color (Beer, 1911, as cited in \citeauthor{klingler_if_2003}, \citeyear[pp.~15-16]{klingler_if_2003}; \citeauthor{hanger_coping_1997}, \citeyear[p.~220]{hanger_coping_1997}).
    The simplest path towards creating a family for White men was thus with enslaved women or free women of color.
    For the women, particularly enslaved women, in a world where they had few options \parencite[p.~65]{martin_placage_2000}, these relationships offered them benefits up to the possibility of freedom for themselves and their offspring, hence becoming free people of color.
    The practice continued through the 18th century and into the 19th century, often facilitated by balls where women would be chosen (\citeauthor{featherstonhaugh_excursion_1968}, \citeyear[pp.~141-142]{featherstonhaugh_excursion_1968}; \citeauthor{latrobe_southern_1986}, \citeyear[p.~77]{latrobe_southern_1986}).
    These were lifelong commitments in some cases, whereas in others they might have lasted only a few years, often ending when the man entered into a legal marriage with a White woman \parencite[pp.~326-327]{martineau_society_1966-1}.

    Children who came out of the \foreign{plaçage} system were of course considered Creoles just as any White people, enslaved people, or free people of color who were born in the colony.
    This use of the term \lexi{Creole} as locally born was still commonly seen in newspapers in the 1820s and 1830s where it was even applied to anglophone Americans and Acadians\footnote{
      Acadians, whose name evolved into Cajun, will be discussed in their own right in Section \ref{sec:cajun_history}.
    } born in Louisiana to those who had been exiled from modern-day Canada \parencite[pp.~23-24]{tregle_early_1952}.
    Despite this ongoing use of Creole to mean local or native, it has also been argued that the Louisiana Purchase in 1803 entailed the beginning of a shift towards having a Creole identity being something that distinguished one from others in the community \parencite[pp.~134-135]{tregle_creoles_1992}.

    The impact of the arrival of Americans on the identity and status of Creoles was perhaps initially felt most directly by free people of color.
    At the outset of the 19th century, free people of color were certainly not the social and legal equals of White people, but they did enjoy significantly more rights than enslaved people, naturally.
    Free people of color were able to obtain an education, use the courts, work, and own property \parencite[p.~34]{tregle_early_1952}, and they were able to trade real estate, boats, lumber, and jewelry \parencite[p.~242]{hanger_avenues_1990}.
    Such trading was lucrative enough for some that they themselves owned enslaved people \parencite[p.~15]{stone_negro_1900}.
    Indeed, in opposition to proposed legislation that would strip the right of free people of color to own property, including owning enslaved people, a newspaper editorial from 1852 argued that free people of color were more disdainful of enslaved people than even White people were and so their involvement in slavery was not a threat to the order of things \parencite[pp.~172-173]{sterkx_free_1972}.
    Although this measure to further lower the status of free people of color failed, others were successful (\foreign{e.g.}, \citeauthor{harrington-bryan_marcus_2000}, \citeyear[p.~52]{harrington-bryan_marcus_2000}; \citeauthor{johnston_race_1970}, \citeyear[p.~231]{johnston_race_1970}).
    Such moves highlight the continual deterioration of the rights of free people of color throughout the 1800s as White people became more and more unsettled by their status, wanting instead to aggregate them with enslaved people.
    These moves also had the effect of undermining the \foreign{plaçage} system by making it more difficult to free enslaved people or for free people of color to obtain inheritances.
    
    Attempts to collapse free people of color and enslaved people into one category intensified further after the Civil War.
    Louisiana was in a state of financial ruin \parencite[pp.~428-429]{winters_civil_1963}, and that combined with the loss of the war and seeing the increased number of free Black people in their midst left White people feeling that their place in the social order was insecure, a sentiment that found regular expression in newspapers \parencite[pp.~173-175]{blassingame_black_1973}.
    Public calls were soon being made for all Louisianians (\foreign{i.e.}, all Creoles) to declare themselves either Black or White and nothing in between.
    For their part, those who were free people of color before slavery ended, being not immune to the change in fortunes in the state, were split on whether to align themselves with formerly enslaved people or with White people \parencite[p.~135-137]{dominguez_white_1986}.
    For White people, though, the choice of how to racialize former free people of color was not a difficult one.

    Whereas the term \lexi{Creole} was applied to White people, free people of color, and enslaved people through much of the 19th century, it was after the war that the ``Creole myth'' came about, asserting that only those of pure White blood were Creoles \parencite{tregle_creoles_1992}.
    In the most innocuous of perpetuations of this myth, one finds clarifying appositions in letters such as one from 1878 that equated Creoles with those ``of French descent'' in contrast to Americans who were those ``of English descent'' \parencite[p.~245]{mccants_they_1970}.
    Something of the old definition still resides in this contrast as American, especially when referred to in F\&C (\foreign{i.e.}, \foreign{les Américains}), is said even today to refer to those who are not from South Louisiana, but the emphasis on French descent suggests a White European identity for Creoles as the other end of that contrast.
    
    In other perpetuations of the myth, there was the addition of the romanticized idea of Creoles having been a sort of aristocratic class.
    \textcite{fortier_french_1884} described Creoles specifically as White people born in the Americas with either French or Spanish heritage, arguing that they ``occupied a high standing'' before the war (p.~98).
    His evidence was a list of successful politicians going back to the second governor of the US state, a list that was in fact in direct contradiction to the original use of the term \lexi{Creole} as it included people like the fifth governor of Louisiana, Pierre Derbigny, who was born in France.
    Finally, some perpetuations of the myth were plainly dehumanizing.
    For example, \textcite{tinker_toucoutou_1928}, writing well after Reconstruction, did not deny that non-White people were described as Creoles but rather claimed that this was always with a lowercase \orth{c} indicating an adjective as could be applied to any type of Louisiana product: carrots, eggs, or in this case people who were considered products (pp.~7-8).
    Of course, Tinker did not provide any evidence for this claim, and it flies in the face of much of what has already been discussed here.

    The Creole myth was likely more successful at retroactively redefining Creoles of the colonial and antebellum periods than in redefining Creoles contemporaneously.
    There were -- and still are -- people who openly identified as Creoles of color in the sense of descending from free people of color when the Creole myth was first propagated.
    By the turn of the century, these Creoles, if they could not pass for White, were subject to Jim Crow laws such as the poll tax that was implemented by the Louisiana constitution of 1898 \parencite[pp.~80-81]{louisiana_constitution_1898}, creating an impetus to attempt to pass.
    
    The practice of passing has been documented up through the 1940s in New Orleans where families would pass for the sake of the success of their businesses.
    Some in the 1970s and 1980s continued the practice, as well, where passing would be carried out sometimes in limited contexts, sometimes in all social contexts, and sometimes with such a forceful assertion of one's Whiteness that one denied one's heritage \parencite[pp.~200-203]{dominguez_white_1986}.
    Situations such as this are why \textcite{martin_placage_2000} argued that the tenuous existence of Creoles of color has created a schism between them and non-Creole Black people that endured up until her time at least (pp.~69-70), perhaps not entirely different from the roles discussed in Section \ref{sec:united_states} for the small Black bourgeoisie in the US, the Pardos in Brazil, and the Coloureds in South Africa.
    
    Such drastic changes in the social and political landscape since the first Creoles came about have resulted in the category and by extension the people being difficult to pin down.
    More recently, researchers have attempted to clarify the definition of Creole by asserting that they are ``multiracial'' people.
    \textcite{susberry_racial_2004} defined them as ``[m]ultiracial people whose ancestral history is connected to Louisiana'' (p.~8).
    \textcite{giancarlo_dont_2019} similarly stated that they are ``mixed-race'' people with French -- and not Spanish -- cultural origins (p.~42).
    These definitions harken back in particular to free people of color who, when born of the \foreign{plaçage} system, were given one of several racialized designations based on the presumed mixture of Black and White ancestors in their genealogies \parencite[p.~35]{tregle_early_1952}.
    By implicating primarily French and Spanish colonial ancestors, what they also suggest is that the White Creole myth has evidently spilled into modern non-White definitions of Creole, as it only began with this myth that Louisiana locals who did not have European ancestors were excluded from being able to identify as Creole.
    
    Other Creoles of color in \citeauthor{susberry_racial_2004}'s (\citeyear{susberry_racial_2004}) study took the term to be more of a cultural concept.
    The implicated cultural features are by and large shared with Cajuns, although neither Cajuns nor Creoles themselves accepted participation in such culture as a sufficient criteria for being Cajun if one was also considered Black (\citeauthor{esman_henderson_1985}, \citeyear[p.~7]{esman_henderson_1985}; \citeauthor{spitzer_cajuns_1977}, \citeyear{spitzer_cajuns_1977}).
    In other words, the existing literature suggests that Cajun has become a specifically White identity.
    It has thus been argued by some that the most common definition for Creoles in recent times is someone with African ancestry and roots in Louisiana \parencite[\foreign{e.g.},][pp.~39-40]{eble_creole_2008}, which is a typical finding when study participants in Louisiana are questioned about their identities.
    \textcite{klingler_language_2003}, for instance, found that all 19 of his participants in St Landry Parish who identified as Creole also identified as Black (p.~82).
    Creole identity is not necessarily strictly exclusionary relative to racialized categories, though.

    Those who in the past might have identified as White Creoles have become much more likely to identify as Cajun.
    Where \lexi{Cajun} had been a derogatory term even up to the mid 1970s, it was transformed into a positive, catch-all term for White South Louisianians by the early 1980s largely through the efforts of the Council for the Development of French in Louisiana (CODOFIL) \parencite[pp.~164-167]{trepanier_cajunization_1991}.
    However, as \textcite{eble_creole_2008} observed, there are Louisianians ``from a wide range of ethnic backgrounds'' who have been attempting to revive the old meaning of Creole as simply native (p.~49).
    Indeed, cultural activists in the state such as Gene Buller and John LaFleur II have rejected the Cajun branding of all things South Louisiana and instead promoted Creole branding at least in part because it is inclusive for those who might otherwise be identified as White and Black people both.
    Their approach is however not a simple revival of Creole as a synonym for native, as Eble claimed, but rather as inclusive of anyone who has European ancestors regardless of whether they also have African ancestors and/or ancestors who were members of indigenous tribes \parencite{partsch_creole_2015}.
    The reclamation is rather a partial disconnection from the racialized implications of the term, partial because it still implicates European hegemony which is a part of what constitutes White hegemony in the US.
      
  \section{The history of Cajuns}
    \label{sec:cajun_history}
    The history of Cajuns in Louisiana is intimately tied to that of the Acadians who resided in what is now Nova Scotia.
    This link exists both in fact and imagination.
    Indeed, the expulsion of the Acadians from Canada in the 18th century, called the \foreign{Grand dérangement} in French, is central to the origin story of the Cajuns of Louisiana today so much so that Henry Wadsworth Longfellow's novel \titlebook{Evangeline}, which romanticized the expulsion of the Acadians from Canada, is well known in South Louisiana for this reason despite the protagonist only spending a very short amount of time in the state \parencite{ross_evangeline_1991}.
    The term \lexi{Cajun} itself is derived from French \lexi{Acadien}, both the form with and without the initial vowel being used at various times in history for various reasons \parencite{henry_acadien_1998}, a fact that any F\&C-speaking Cajun today will likely be able to reiterate.

    The Acadians themselves had European roots, having arrived in North America from mainly the western parts of France \parencite{massignon_parlers_1962}, after which it is quite likely that they intermingled with the Mi'kmaq who already resided in the area around Port Royal, their first settlement, in 1605 (\citeauthor{griffiths_contexts_1992}, \citeyear[pp.~22-25]{griffiths_contexts_1992}; \citeauthor{massignon_parlers_1962}, \citeyear[p.~17]{massignon_parlers_1962}).
    France lost the region to the British is 1713, and by 1755, the British decided to forcibly exile the Acadians for their refusal to swear allegiance to Britain \parencite{faragher_great_2005}.
    Not all of these Acadians ended up in Louisiana, and none arrived there directly.
    A significant contingent had first returned to France from which they were once again deported to Spanish-controlled Louisiana with between 1,200 and 1,500 others arriving later.
    They initially settled in the Attakapas, the Acadian Coast along the Mississippi, and Natchez, which covers a large span of South Louisiana, roughly 250 miles (\citeauthor{brasseaux_foundings_1987}, \citeyear[pp.~74-78/91-100]{brasseaux_foundings_1987}; \citeauthor{winzerling_acadian_1955}, \citeyear[p.~109]{winzerling_acadian_1955}).
    Being able to connect one's lineage to some degree to these early families was often important for those who identified as Cajun up to at least the end of the 20th century \parencite[p.~184]{ryon_conflit_2000}, although those lines of descent are not often straightforward, as will be discussed shortly.
      
    Whereas the Creole myth discussed in Section \ref{sec:creole_history} characterized Creoles of the past as aristocratic, the picture of Cajuns has been consistently that of an impoverished people.
    In his journey through Louisiana in the first half of the 19th century, \textcite{olmsted_journey_1904} was told by his travel companions that the ``Acadians'' whose ``hamlet of cottages'' they passed were ``poor white, French Creoles'' (p.~342).
    \citeauthor{tregle_early_1952} (\citeyear[pp.~23-24]{tregle_early_1952}, \citeyear{tregle_that_1982}) had additionally shown that Acadian governor Alexandre Mouton was also described as Creole in the press.
    This use of \lexi{Creole} for various Acadians was of course consistent with its original use.
    Importantly, however, Cajuns were not considered to be rich Creoles at all even if some could reach the status of governor.
    Accounts made by traveling Northerners after the war described ``Acadians'' as white trash, poor, uneducated, unambitious, and even primitive to the point where, by the late 19th century, Americans used the term \lexi{Cajun} to describe any poor person of French descent \parencite[pp.~101-104]{brasseaux_acadian_1992}.
    \lexi{Cajun} had become such a derogatory word that \textcite{ralph_along_1893}, during his travels in Louisiana, was warned not to call (White) Acadians in Bayou Teche ``Cajun'' without the \orth{A} as this would be considered an insult (p.~883).
    
    While it is clear that a stigma was being attached to Cajuns already by the 19th century based on their low socioeconomic status, especially when using the localized version of their name, Cajuns were not an entirely homogenous group.
    Even in terms of their financial situations, while many were poor, some were well-off enough to own enslaved people.
    The first such documentation of this was as early as the 1770s \parencite[p.~191]{brasseaux_foundings_1987}.
    Census records from the 1850s in Lafayette Parish suggest that this practice continued and became relatively normal for Cajuns, although most who owned enslaved people owned very few of them \parencite{baker_patterns_1974}.
    Additionally, while it has been argued that Cajuns in the 19th century were not very successful at forming sociopolitical alliances with other groups \parencite[p.~27]{johnson_louisiana_1976}, exogamous marriages did occur.
    Cajun women outnumbered Cajun men up to at least the Civil War and so often married non-Cajun men.
    In Terrebonne-Lafourche in the southeastern portion of the state, as many as 40\% of the Cajun women who married chose men from outside of their group, such as Germans.
    As a result, their family names changed to non-Acadian names or even non-French names, yet their children were still raised as Cajuns \parencite[pp.~38-39/106]{brasseaux_acadian_1992}.

    By the early 20th century, the image of Cajuns as poor was still the primary one.
    For example, \textcite{smith_influence_1939} argued that Cajuns lacked mobility largely due to their status as poor farmers (p.~198).
    Immobility and isolation had in fact become central to characterizations of Cajuns by that time to such an extent that it was argued that this had led them to be ``probably the largest unassimilated nationality group in America'' \parencite[p.~82]{gilmore_social_1933}.
    It is doubtful that Gilmore's use of ``nationality'' for Cajuns was meant to imply that they were a politically organized ethnic group in his view, however, as sociologists use the term today, but rather to imply that Cajuns were culturally foreigners in relation to other Americans.
    As far as \textcite{smith_influence_1939} were concerned, that nationality was French as they highlighted both being francophone and being ``of French descent,'' be it Acadian or otherwise, in their definition of Cajuns (p.~198).

    While Acadian ancestry never quite disappeared as a commonly expressed criteria for Cajun membership, cultural factors came to be highlighted more by the 1970s.
    For \textcite{del_sesto_culture_1975}, for instance, Cajuns were Louisianians who were Catholic, rural, who privileged kinship relationships and spoke or at least knew speakers of French.
    They explicitly suggested that Cajuns were not only those whose ancestors were expelled from Acadia but also others who had been incorporated into the group over time (p.~3).
    \textcite{brown_pronominal_1988} went even further, claiming that ``someone who calls himself a Cajun today is no longer necessarily of Acadian ancestry,'' basing this on all of her participants identifying as Cajun even though those who settled their parish of Avoyelles, assumed to be her participants' ancestors, were thought to have come directly from Europe (pp.~18-20/22).
    It has in fact been shown that Avoyelles was just north of where the Acadians initially settled \parencite{brasseaux_foundings_1987}.
    This does not mean that Acadians never spread north over time, however, which exemplifies the murkiness of using ancestry to establish Cajun membership and perhaps the reason why genealogy is such a prevalent topic for Louisianians today.

    What is clear, though, is that self-identifications as Cajun have become more attractive and easier to have recognized by others since the late 20th century.
    For one, the stigma that was once attached to Cajuns has transformed into pride since the 1970s through what has been dubbed the Cajun Renaissance.
    This movement was initially referred to as a ``renaissance du français'' \parencite[p.~40]{smith-thibodeaux_francophones_1977} or a ``renaissance française''\footnote{
      Renaissance of French or French Renaissance, respectively
    } \parencite[p.~205]{waddell_louisiane_1979}.
    Much of the initial revalorization efforts were focused on the French language in Louisiana \parencite{smith-thibodeaux_francophones_1977}, a variety that was branded Cajun to the exclusion of Creoles, people of various local tribes, and others, just as their role has been excluded from many other aspects of Louisiana culture \parencite{giancarlo_dont_2019}.
    The Renaissance itself has come to be labeled Cajun (\foreign{e.g.}, \citeauthor{dubois_when_1999}, \citeyear[p.~305]{dubois_when_1999}; \citeauthor{glain_cajun_2021}, \citeyear{glain_cajun_2021}), perhaps as a result of the diminished role of F\&C in the state as will be discussed further in Section \ref{sec:fc_status}.
    As an example of this diminished role for defining Cajun identities, \textcite{ryon_conflit_2000} found that, among college students at the University of Louisiana, Lafayette, French fluency was a sufficient criteria for Cajun membership, especially among men, but not a necessary criteria as 63\% of men and 42\% of women who identified as Cajun did not speak French with their families (pp.~180-182).
    Similarly, \citeauthor{tornquist_attitudes_2000}'s (\citeyear{tornquist_attitudes_2000}) survey of parents of French immersion students showed that 58\% of the parents who identified as Cajun spoke French (p.~188), meaning 42\% of the Cajun parents who valued the language enough to send their children to French immersion schools were unable to speak the language themselves.
    While efforts to re-establish pride in French in Louisiana started with the language, as the language continued to decline in importance, this newfound pride was instead projected on to Cajun identity in general, helping to sustain a movement that has been successful enough that, by the 1990s, \textcite{rottet_language_1995} felt comfortable in concluding that the Cajuns of Terrebonne-Lafourche with whom he worked were no longer stigmatized (p.~110).

    Today, cultural factors have continued to be used for defining Cajuns, but Cajun is also discussed as something along the lines of a panethnicity \parencite{omi_racial_1997}.
    \textcite{dajko_sociolinguistics_2012} suggested that one could be Cajun through associations with foodways, music, the French language, ``and until recently, the Catholic faith,'' all of which she claimed could also be used as valid criteria for Creole membership (p.~280).
    Likewise, \citeauthor{ryon_conflit_2000}'s (\citeyear{ryon_conflit_2000}) college students also reported foodways and religion as important aspects of Cajun identity (p.~184).
    The similarity in what cultural features can be used for establishing Cajun and Creole membership is notable, however, as it reduces the potential for cultural factors to delineate the two groups and thus provides room for categories from the broader US that have been racialized to be applied to these local categories.
    As already discussed in section \ref{sec:creole_history}, for many, Cajun has come to be more or less a synonym for White and Creole for non-White with exceptions.
    On the Cajun side, while being inclusive of essentially all White South Louisianians has perhaps provided the group with greater robustness and sociopolitical power, as is sometimes the motivation for other groups that come together to form a panethnicity, as \textcite{giancarlo_dont_2019} put it, it also ``minimizes a diversity of white ethnicities'' (p.~33), essentially through a form of erasure \parencite{irvine_language_2000}.
    As one of \citeauthor{trepanier_cajunization_1991}'s (\citeyear{trepanier_cajunization_1991}) respondents from Breaux Bridge resentfully stated in 1981, ``On s'appelait des Créoles avant cette affaire de Cadjin''\footnote{
      We called ourselves Creoles before this Cajun business.
    } (p.~167).
    While all those born in South Louisiana were at one time Creoles, including Cajuns, a realignment has occurred where these two categories are mutually exclusive and appear to be more, though not categorically, in accord with the broader US binary of White versus non-White.
        
  \section{The status of F\&C in Louisiana}
    \label{sec:fc_status}
    Fluency in French or Creole is a factor that can facilitate recognition as Cajun or Creole by others for those who self-identify as such, but language ideologies surrounding these varieties are also entangled with Creole and Cajun identities.
    The glottonyms Creoles and Cajuns choose for what they speak depend as much on self-identifications as the structural qualities of their speech.
    In St Landry Parish, an area from which much of the data for the present study comes, it was found that those who identified as Cajuns called their language French, whereas those who identified as Creoles called their language Creole even when some Creoles used linguistic features typically marked as French and some Cajuns used linguistic features typically marked as Creole \parencite{klingler_language_2003}.
    In light of these intimate connections with identity and language in Louisiana, the linguistic focus of the present study is on F\&C and more specifically subject pronouns in F\&C.
    Its status in the state is therefore important for understanding the context in which participants speak it.
    As will be seen, a language shift \parencite{fishman_reversing_1991} to English dominance in Louisiana has occurred, and F\&C has in many ways become more of a symbolic resource than an instrument for communication, what one might call a post-vernacular language \parencite{reershemius_post-vernacular_2009, shandler_adventures_2006}.

    The arrival of French in Louisiana started with the colonization of the region by the French.
    This early group of Europeans have been dubbed the \foreign{ancienne population}\footnote{Old/former population}, who were followed by the arrival of several other major francophone groups at different times: the \foreign{Français étrangers}\footnote{Foreign French}, the Acadians, and those coming from Saint-Domingue\footnote{Present-day Haiti} \parencite[p.~19]{johnson_louisiana_1976}.
    Indeed, while the bulk of early colonists came from Île-de-France\footnote{The region of Paris and its surrounding area} and Brittany \parencite[pp.~15-17]{brasseaux_french_2005}, immigration of French speakers has been claimed to have continued even after the Louisiana Purchase up to the Civil War \parencite[p.~99]{fortier_french_1884}. % No source for any of these facts. Verify in Brasseaux 1990 maybe if you can get your hands on another physical copy
    This includes the period of Spanish rule from 1762 to 1800.
    As already covered in Section \ref{sec:cajun_history}, the Acadians arrived in Louisiana primarily during this time of Spanish rule, but so did those who immigrated in response to the French Revolution in France\footnote{
      The bulk of this latter group came just after 1800 when Spain had relinquished control of Louisiana, but it started while Spain was still in power.
    } (\citeauthor{brasseaux_french_2005}, \citeyear[p.~22]{brasseaux_french_2005}; \citeauthor{lachance_new_1989}, \citeyear{lachance_new_1989}).

    It is often maintained at least implicitly that Acadian French is the primary origin of the variety of French spoken in rural Louisiana, in line with the popular Cajun origin story.
    This idea has been challenged at least when applied to the entirety of South Louisiana \parencite{klingler_how_2009}, mostly along demographic lines as Acadians made up a very small percentage of the population in some parishes such as Avoyelles and Pointe Coupee \parencite[p.~107]{brasseaux_acadian_1992}.
    However, the numbers are essentially reversed in other regions \parencite[p.~168]{brasseaux_acadian_1992}.
    While it is possible that Acadian solidarity gave them an outsized linguistic influence in both scenarios \parencite[pp.~21-22]{brown_pronominal_1988}, immigration from elsewhere cannot be ignored.
    For instance, over 10,000 people arrived in New Orleans from Saint-Domingue in 1809 and 1810 \parencite[p.~247]{lachance_1809_1992}, most of whom would presumably be francophones and/or creolophones\footnote{
      In examining the lyrics of songs that were recorded in New Orleans in 1937, \textcite{klingler_creole_2024} presented some preliminary indications that an earlier form of Haitian Creole was in fact brought to the city.
    }.
    This latter group did not remain entirely in New Orleans as there is evidence of them settling in various places across rural South Louisiana as far west and north as Natchitoches \parencite[p.~23]{fiehrer_tortue_1992}, which would have put them in contact with Acadians and any earlier colonists already in those areas.
    The Acadians of rural South Louisiana were undoubtedly an important component of the early developments in French but several other components fill out the full picture.

    By all accounts, French, Creole, and English were all widely spoken in Louisiana throughout the 19th century, the former thanks to factors such as this continued francophone immigration.
    Despite the Louisiana Constitution of 1812 making English the official language of legislation \parencite{bush_first_1975}, many legislators were monolingual French speakers, so not only was French tolerated during deliberations \parencite[p.~33]{smith-thibodeaux_francophones_1977}, but the 1845 constitution required the Secretary of the Senate and the Clerk of the House of Representatives to be bilingual \parencite[p.~12]{louisiana_constitution_1845}.
    French-language newspapers were also widespread, indicating that literacy was not uncommon among the populace.
    They began being published in 1794 and mostly ended by 1900, although some continued up until 1955 (\citeauthor{johnson_louisiana_1976}, \citeyear[p.~22]{johnson_louisiana_1976}; \citeauthor{klingler_if_2003}, \citeyear[p.~108]{klingler_if_2003}).
    French was so ever-present in writing and day-to-day interactions that language ideologies towards different varieties developed that were at times explicitly laid out.
    For instance, in general, \textcite{fortier_french_1884} found the French of Louisiana to have a certain ``purity'' which he believed to be due to the men of rich families having almost always been educated in France ``during the old regime.''
    For those who could not afford such educations, such as Cajuns in particular, he described their speech as ``not very elegant,'' containing features such as the use of \lexi{je} as a first person plural subject pronoun rather than \lexi{nous} or even \lexi{on}, \lexi{paré} for \gloss{ready} rather than \lexi{prêt}, and \lexi{il mouille} for \gloss{it is raining} rather than \lexi{il pleut} (p.~99).
    Just as being a Cajun in the 19th century had become akin to being poor and uneducated, so was their language considered.

    The decline of French in Louisiana, however, occurred most rapidly in New Orleans rather than in the rural areas populated by Cajuns.
    Although his evidence was not robust, \textcite{johnson_louisiana_1976} claimed that the last holdouts for the language in the city were unemployed wives up until World War I (WWI), and that by 1970, there were perhaps no more than 5,000 ``indigenous native French speakers'' (pp.~22-23).
    By contrast, long after WWI, some had guessed that as much as 90\% of the population of rural South Louisiana spoke French \parencite[p.~198]{smith_influence_1939}.
    Regardless of the accuracy of such a guess, this number would also fall sharply during the 20th century.
    The Louisiana Constitution of 1921 forbade the use of French as the language of instruction in regular classrooms \parencite[p.~93]{louisiana_constitution_1921}, but some researchers have pointed to the 1940s as the decade when a strong shift towards English truly took route in the rural areas as a result of the expansion of public education and the arrival of anglophone North Louisianians and Texans who took up all of the new jobs in the petroleum industry (\citeauthor{larouche_ethnicite_1980}, \citeyear[pp.~117-134]{larouche_ethnicite_1980}; \citeauthor{western_social_1971}, \citeyear[pp.~35-36]{western_social_1971}).
    Essentially, F\&C came to be stigmatized and seen as a handicap to one's advancement in the world.

    In various parts of Louisiana and in succeeding decades, this pattern of decline has been repeatedly documented.\footnote{
      Some caution does need to be taken with speaker estimates as they are often based on census data.
      Census questions have, however, changed over time or have been misunderstood.
      For instance, the 1970 census asked respondents to identify their childhood home language, whereas the 1990 census asked them to identify the language that was currently being spoken in their home \parencite[pp.~112-113]{rottet_language_1995}.
      For misunderstanding the questions, \textcite{brown_social_1993} reported that some of her informants read ``French'' as meaning ``International French'' rather than simply any variety of French (p.~98).
    } Among survey respondents in Evangeline Parish and Pointe Coupee Parish in the 1960s, 37.5\% reported speaking only French\footnote{
      \textcite{bertrand_french_1965} did not employ the glottonym \lexi{Creole}, but given the prevalence of especially speech in Pointe Coupee being documentary as having ``Creole'' structures (\citeauthor{klingler_if_2003}, \citeyear{klingler_if_2003}; \citeauthor{klingler_language_2003}, \citeyear[p.~81]{klingler_language_2003}; \citeauthor{rottet_language_1995}, \citeyear[p.~7]{rottet_language_1995}), it is likely that at least some of these respondents were treating \lexi{French} as referring to any French-based language used in the area.
    } at home during their childhoods whereas 53.5\% reported speaking only English and 9.0\% reported speaking both.
    For those who reported speaking only French as children, 68\% still spoke it frequently as adults \parencite[pp.~18-19]{bertrand_french_1965}, suggesting that it was not being retained by all individuals over their lifetimes.
    By 1970, the US census indicated that 572,262 Louisianians had French as their childhood home language (\citeauthor{johnson_louisiana_1976}, \citeyear[pp.~19/36]{johnson_louisiana_1976}; \citeauthor{neumann-holzschuh_creole_1985}, \citeyear[p.~16]{neumann-holzschuh_creole_1985}), although CODOFIL itself had given a much higher and more optimistic estimate of 1.5 million around that same time \parencite[p.~36]{johnson_louisiana_1976}.
    Later in that decade, \textcite{smith-thibodeaux_francophones_1977} provided a rough guess of 300,000 to 500,000 French-speakers in Louisiana based on reflections from CODOFIL's previous estimate and national census records (p.~43).
    The linguistic situation was such that by \citeauthor{del_sesto_culture_1975}'s (\citeyear{del_sesto_culture_1975}) time, they suspected that there were still monolingual French speakers, but they were not able to find any (p.~7).

    The 1980s saw more documentation of the decline of F\&C, including one of the few examples of an estimate for creolophones.
    \textcite{neumann-holzschuh_creole_1985} reasoned that there were between 60,000 and 80,000 creolophones in Louisiana using census data and supposing that a certain proportion of those Louisianians who identified as Black and francophone in fact spoke Creole (p.~20).
    Stable bilingualism was also noted to be the rule in the towns of Pigeon and Pierre Part and the lower portions of Terrebonne-Lafourche, whereas in all other areas it was considered to be a sign of language shift.
    That language shift was evident in surveys of South Louisiana that showed that only 41\% of respondents said that their strongest language was French, whereas 92\% believed that was their grandparents' strongest language, 84\% believed that was their parents' strongest language, and a meager 3\% felt that was their children's strongest language \parencite[pp.~273/283]{trepanier_french_1988}.
    CODOFIL later carried out a survey of eight parishes that estimated that 40.6\% of the population of Acadiana spoke French, 42.1\% of those being over 45 years old and 25.0\% being under 19 \parencite[pp.~4-5]{henry_espoir_1990}, again suggesting a clear downward trend.
    By the 1990s, even in rural Terrebonne-Lafourche where bilingualism had been described as the norm, meaning that one might expect some individuals to be able to lead French monolingual lives, \textcite{rottet_language_1995} reported that he was unable to find a single monolingual French speaker (p.~162).

    Part of the reason that F\&C has been maintained in South Louisiana at all may be related to isolation, especially considering how quickly unisolated New Orleans became anglophone, as has been observed for some but not all minority language communities \parencite{mufwene_ecology_1997, schilling-estes_nature_2002, schilling-estes_alternative_1999}.
    This argument has been put forth specifically for Cajuns as 60\% of them lived in rural areas in 1970 \parencite[pp.~27-28]{johnson_louisiana_1976}, and highways connecting these areas to population hubs were lacking in the early 20th century \parencite[p.~268]{gold_french_1979}.
    In some low-lying areas, highways that would connect them to the rest of the state were not completed until the 1950s, and I-10, perhaps the most significant interstate highway passing through Louisiana, was not finished until 1975 \parencite[pp.~26-27]{brown_pronominal_1988}.
    Isolation can also be social, however, as in the example of how well the Houma in Terrebonne-Lafourche maintained their use of French.
    During the era of formal segregation, White and Black schools both existed but many Houma in these parishes did not go to either as they were not allowed into the White schools and were not willing to attend the Black schools \parencite[Roy, 1959, as cited in][pp.~61-62]{rottet_language_1995}, meaning the influence of English-language instruction had less of an impact on them as a result of their educational isolation \parencite{bromham_global_2022, skutnabb-kangas_linguistic_2000}.
    Despite the disadvantages of physical isolation and the ills of segregation, both inadvertently helped perpetuate the use of French.
    However, as isolation broke down in the wake of compulsory education, the oil boom, World War II (WWII), and mass communication, South Louisiana became more Americanized, and as has been argued, with that Americanization came a greater propensity towards English (\citeauthor{bernard_cajuns_2003}, \citeyear{bernard_cajuns_2003}; \citeauthor{conrad_acadians_1978}, \citeyear[p.~15]{conrad_acadians_1978}; \citeauthor{landry_creole_2016}, \citeyear{landry_creole_2016}; \citeauthor{neumann-holzschuh_creole_1985}, \citeyear[p.~49]{neumann-holzschuh_creole_1985}).

    \subsection{Domains of use}
      \label{sec:domain_indicators}
      Besides raw numbers, the status of F\&C can also be examined via its presence or absence in various domains.
      Not only do domains of use constitute part of \citeauthor{fishman_reversing_1991}'s (\citeyear{fishman_reversing_1991}) model for measuring language shift, understanding these domains also helps contextualize speakers' sociolinguistic lives.
      In the case of Louisiana, outside of the home, French is primarily visible in education.
      For other domains such as politics and religion, French has either disappeared or only has a moderate presence.
      Its use in music and radio has been more successful and consistent.
      In the later parts of the 20th century, French literature has also shown some signs of returning, and most recently, French has also obtained some presence in visual media.
      Much of this is indicative of post-vernacularity in that the use of the language is now more of a performance than a regular, daily means of communication \parencite{shandler_adventures_2006}.

      Despite the ban on the use of French for regular instruction in 1921, French was still offered as an elective in high schools \parencite[p.~199]{smith_influence_1939} and continues to be offered into the present.
      Schools technically had to offer five years of French in 1970, in fact, although exceptions could be granted via a simple request \parencite[p.~106]{rottet_language_1995}.
      These requests seem likely to have been regularly made as, by the late 20th century, high school students were reporting that they had little access to French classes in their schools \parencite[p.~463]{landry_french_1996}.
      CODOFIL was largely responsible for recruiting teachers for French language courses where they were offered, at one point bringing in an estimated 200 foreign francophone instructors per year who taught roughly 40,000 students.
      Importantly, they employed textbooks brought from France due to the limited local French pedagogical materials that were available \parencite[pp.~27-28]{johnson_louisiana_1976}.
      One of the few attempts at creating a textbook based on local varieties of F\&C did not come until the 1970s.
      \citeauthor{faulk_cajun_1977}'s (\citeyear{faulk_cajun_1977}) textbook was based on the ``Cajun French'' of Vermilion Parish, but unfortunately, as many native speakers in Louisiana were illiterate in French by the mid 20th century \parencite[p.~158]{dubois_attrition_2001}, Faulk employed a completely new orthography that was based on written English rather than standard French orthography, hence his textbook was not an ideal substitute for textbooks from France or at the very least could not help connect Louisianian francophones to the written French world outside of the state.
      While using these latter materials from France did not necessarily create comprehension problems, it acted to stigmatize local speech patterns in the minds of at least some Louisianians \parencite[p.~77]{brown_social_1993}.
      This has been an issue in other locations, such as the Caribbean \parencite{le_page_problems_1968}, where teaching a dominant, standard variety acts as a ``rejection of the norms of home usage'' \parencite[p.~184]{le_page_acts_1985}, but the sentiment has continued to be echod up until recent times, notably in raciolinguistic work \parencite{flores_undoing_2015}.

      French immersion programs in Louisiana have naturally had similar issues with materials and stigmatization of local speech norms due to teachers coming primarily from other countries, although teachers from Louisiana did become more common eventually.
      According to a former CODOFIL director, immersion programs began in 1979 in Baton Rouge, and by 1994 there were programs in the parishes of Lafayette, Orleans, Assumption, Calcasieu, and St Martin.
      Estimates from 1993 suggest that there were 80,000 immersion school students with about 79\% of the teachers being native Louisianians \parencite[p.~107]{rottet_language_1995}.
      By 2008 in Lafayette Parish, French immersion was available for full days from kindergarten to 5th grade and for half days from 6th grade to 8th grade \parencite[p.~67]{lindner_attitudes_2008}.
      Some 900 students were enrolled in the program by that time \parencite{tutwiler_french_2006}.
      There are, however, no indications that any of these programs or local instructors employed Creole in the classroom.

      Politics has presented a mostly symbolic presence of French.
      Some early to mid 20th century politicians spoke French publicly, such as Dudley LeBlanc, active from the 1930s onward \parencite{clay_coozan_1973}, and Edwin Edwards, who in 1971 became the first French-speaking governor since the Civil War \parencite[p.~29]{johnson_louisiana_1976}, but these politicians' sporadic use of French was merely an exception to the rule of English dominance in politics.
      More recently, state legislators Stephen Ortego and Eric Lafleur tried with some moderate success to shine a light on French.\footnote{
        The prime example was Act No.~263, which gave localities the right to replace English highway signs with English-French bilingual signs \parencite{ortego_act_2014}, although I am aware of no locality which has taken advantage of that law.
      }
      The former lost his campaign for re-election in 2015, and the latter reached his term limit in 2019, leaving Dewith Carrier, first elected in 2019, and Jeremy Stine, first elected in 2021, as the only Louisiana state legislators who make any public use of French \parencite{tele-louisiane_louisiana_2024}.\footnote{
        Stine's French was the product of the French immersion system in Louisiana, whereas Carrier learned French at home.
      }
      
      With the loss of monolingual francophones by the end of the 20th century, F\&C is never used out of necessity in the political domain and likewise for non-governmental institutions such as churches.
      From 1921 to 1945, 34\% of church services in Acadiana held services in French, but that number shrank to just 3\% by 1981 \parencite[p.~238]{trepanier_french_1988}.
      Today, St Bernard Catholic Church in Breaux Bridge holds mass in French on the second Saturday of each month \parencite{noauthor_st_nodate}, and St Martin de Tours Catholic Church in St Martinville holds mass in French at least once a year but only occasionally beyond that \parencite{noauthor_sainte_nodate}.

      In contrast, two areas where French has had a notable presence for much of the 20th century up until the present are music and radio.
      The first Cajun music recordings, for which singing in French is a primary characteristic \parencite[p.~76]{mcneill_use_2014}, were made in the 1920s beginning with those of Cléoma Falcon (née Breaux) \parencite[p.~14]{brasseaux_cajun_2009}.
      The music itself played an important role in the Cajun Renaissance as fiddler and activist Dewey Balfa, upon returning from the Newport Folk Festival in the mid 1960s with a new sense of pride in his culture, began successfully promoting what was at that time a stigmatized musical form \parencite[p.~289]{ancelet_cajun_1994}.
      The reverberations of those efforts exist today as new music in the genre is still recorded in French even by young musicians such as the Huvals and Les Amis du Têche.
      Live Cajun music can thus easily be found throughout the region and can also be heard on the radio.
      French-language radio began in Louisiana in 1943 \parencite[p.~182]{brasseaux_cajun_2009} and has continued to the present day with stations such as a morning talk show on KVPI in Ville Platte and a number of music programs on KRVS in Lafayette.
      With modern technology, these broadcasts are more accessible than ever through the internet.

      Visual mediums, however, have been less proliferated until more recently.
      Télévision-Louisiane was also established through the law that created CODOFIL, Act No.~458 \parencite{louisiana_acts_1968}, but it was not until 1980 that the program's first series was realized with the talk show \titlebook{En Français} appearing on Louisiana Public Broadcasting (LPB) (\citeauthor{noauthor_en_nodate}, \citeyear{noauthor_en_nodate}; \citeauthor{henry_louisiana_1997}, \citeyear[p.~199]{henry_louisiana_1997}).
      While the program ran until 1993, no others were developed by CODOFIL during that time or since.
      In 2018, however, the independent media platform Télé-Louisiane was launched and has produced series ranging from the children's animated show \titlebook{Boudini et ses amis} to news programs like \titlebook{La Veillée}, broadcasting online and on LPB \parencite{noauthor_tele-louisiane_nodate}.
      Of note is the Télé-Louisiane program \titlebook{LACréole Show}, hosted by a Talib Auguste, a young Creole who is a native speaker of both Creole and French.
      Whereas programming in French has been relatively rare, programming in Creole has been completely absent previously as language maintenance and revitalization efforts have historically excluded Creole from the conversation.

      A creative domain with a somewhat more mixed record is that of literature and writing in general, undoubtedly due to the francophone population becoming largely illiterate in the language in the 20th century \parencite[p.~158]{dubois_attrition_2001}.
      In Terrebonne-Lafourche, \textcite{rottet_language_1995} reported that only 10 of his 71 participants claimed to read French at least rarely (p.~146), and by \citeauthor{blainey_first_2013}'s (\citeyear{blainey_first_2013}) time in Golden Meadow, also part of Lafourche Parish, only 2 of her 33 participants were literate in French thanks to some ``very limited'' self-taught knowledge (p.~71).
      This would help explain why the last French-language newspaper \titlebook{Le Courrier de la Nouvelle-Orléans} struggled and finally ceased being printed in 1955 as the audience that existed earlier was no longer there.
      
      Locally produced literature in French did in fact exist before the 20th century.
      This began with the play \titlebook{Poucha Houmma} by Paul Leblanc de Villeneufve in 1814 and continued throughout the century with notable works such as Alfred Mercier's novel \titlebook{L'Habitation Saint-Ybars} in 1881 \parencite[pp.~32/55]{fortier_french_1886}, but the 20th century was mostly devoid of francophone literature save for the various collections of folk stories and poetry written or edited by Barry Ancelet, sometimes under the pseudonym Jean Arceneaux.
      An example of one such poetry collection is \titlebook{Cris sur le bayou} published in 1980, which despite one scholar almost 20 years later hailing it as ``the birth of Cajun poetry,'' that same scholar also concluded that readership was too small to keep these works in print let alone print new works \parencite{guenin-lelle_birth_1997}.
      Nonetheless, the 21st century has seen something of a boom for written poetry especially, mostly through the publisher Les Éditions Tintamarre at Centenary College of Louisiana.
      One important recent work is Kirby Jambon's book of poems \titlebook{Petites Communions} which found further validation through being honored by the Académie française in France when it awarded him the Prix Henri de Régnier in 2014 \parencite{noauthor_kirby_nodate}.
      Important for a different reason is the collection of poems from various writers published in 2022 entitled \titlebook{Févi}.
      What makes this work stand out is that it is entirely and explicitly in Creole rather than French, even employing a distinct Creole writing system that was developed in 2016 \parencite{landry_kouri-vini_2016}.
      Once again, Creole is something of an afterthought when it comes to attempts to valorize F\&C, consistent with its association with those placed at the bottom of the social hierarchy.

    \subsection{Acknowledging Louisiana Creole}
      \label{sec:acknowledging_creole}
      The previous section made ample references to ``French'' but not to ``F\&C''.
      This is not an oversight, as Louisiana Creole has received very little positive attention neither historically nor in the 20th and 21st centuries.
      The focus has indeed been on French, whether a local variety or a foreign variety, whether it is television programming, radio programming, religious services, political statements, legislation, or literature.
      Some historical exceptions include the previously mentioned novel \titlebook{L'Habitation Saint-Ybars} and a collection of folktales written down by Alcée Fortier \parencite{fortier_louisiana_2011}, both from the 19th century.
      The former was written in French but employed Creole dialogue spoken by the enslaved and formerly enslaved characters as well as the children who were effectively raised by these characters and so were imagined to be able to speak both French and Creole.
      This portrait draws a clear association between Creole and the marginalized.
      The latter was entirely in Creole but written by a man who expressed all the ``condescending and paternalistic'' views towards its speakers as might be expected from one who was raised on a plantation where those speakers were enslaved \parencite[pp.~xi-xii]{desmond_introduction_2011}.
      The more modern-day exceptions include the previously mentioned television interview program \titlebook{LACréole Show} and the book of poetry \titlebook{Févi}.
      As is perhaps obvious from the dearth of exceptions presented here, they are exceedingly rare.
      Even CODOFIL's website, which had once had Creole added as a viewing language in addition to English and French \parencite{noauthor_homepage_2025}, had removed Creole as an option when it was redesigned in September 2025 so that only English and French were available once again \parencite{noauthor_homepage_2025-1}.

      Even efforts to obtain estimates of the number of creolophones in the state are sorely lacking, and those that do exist are only inferred.
      \textcite{neumann-holzschuh_creole_1985} relied on census data about racialized categories and language to arrive at her estimate (p.~20), and \citeauthor{klingler_probleme_2005}'s (\citeyear{klingler_probleme_2005}) much more recent estimate of 30,000 does not seem to be based on anything other than a rough guess from personal experience (p.~351).
      While these two researchers have been instrumental in documenting Creole for academic posterity, those who would have the resources to answer basic questions such as the number of speakers are far more interested in applying their energy to French only.
      This situation itself speaks to a social hierarchy where Creoles and their speech patterns are marginalized.

      It is with this marginalization in mind that an acknowledgement of Louisiana Creole and its origins feels appropriate.
      What makes creole languages sociolinguistically interesting are the extreme social circumstances that bring them about.
      Indeed, while some creolists have insisted on the theory that creoles are born of pidgins and/or can be identified by their linguistic structures (\foreign{e.g.}, \citeauthor{bakker_creoles_2011}, \citeyear{bakker_creoles_2011}; \citeauthor{mcwhorter_linguistic_2011} \citeyear[pp.~31-39]{mcwhorter_linguistic_2011}; \citeauthor{parkvall_creoles_2018}, \citeyear{parkvall_creoles_2018}), this is likely a minority view today.
      For instance, \textcite{aboh_null_2017} explicitly rejected the idea that creoles will share linguistic structures due to their status as creoles and instead argued that their structural characteristics are always based on the languages in contact that give rise to the creoles in question (pp.~413/415), but that contact must involve particular demographics, social relationships, and community settings (\foreign{i.e.}, the subjugation of one people by another) (\citeauthor{ansaldo_deconstructing_2007}, \citeyear[p.~8]{ansaldo_deconstructing_2007}; \citeauthor{bartens_creole_2013}, \citeyear[p.~65]{bartens_creole_2013}; \citeauthor{mintz_socio-historical_1971}, \citeyear[p.~481]{mintz_socio-historical_1971}).
      This typically means that slavery or slave-like conditions must exist, the former being the case in Louisiana.
      
      Slavery was practiced in Louisiana almost as soon as Europeans arrived and lasted up to the Civil War.
      Between 1717 and 1721, the first enslaved Africans were brought to the colony, most being Bambara whereas some others were Wolof or Serer \parencite[pp.~32-33]{usner_indians_1992}.
      These people were trafficked from the Senegal Concession in Africa, which ran from Sierra Leone in the south to Mauritania in the north, and numbered 5,951 \parencite[p.~60]{hall_africans_1992}.
      Their numbers held fairly even with those of Europeans so that in 1726, enslaved people made up 41\% of the population and 60\% in 1732 \parencite[p.~46]{usner_indians_1992}.
      During Spanish rule from 1762 to 1800, there was no reprieve from slavery.
      In fact, the number of enslaved people more than quadrupled from about 4,600 to about 24,000 \parencite[p.~279]{hall_africans_1992}.
      Of the roughly 55\% of the people who arrived during Spanish rule and whose origins are known, 55\% came from Africa, 37\% were Creoles in the sense that they were born in Louisiana, and 4\% came from English-speaking regions \parencite[pp.~20-21/24]{klingler_if_2003}.
      This pattern naturally changed after the Louisiana Purchase as the foreign importation of enslaved people was banned one year later in 1804.
      The ban was not on slavery itself, however, as enslaved people could still be trafficked from other parts of the US to Louisiana if they were born before 1798 and were owned by Americans \parencite[pp.~179-180]{lachance_politics_1989}.
      The importation of anglophone enslaved people is thought to be why, in some areas such as Pointe Coupee, there are more White people who speak Creole than non-White people as the enslaved Creoles would have had more and earlier exposure to English and incentive to learn it in order to communicate with these newcomers \parencite[p.~108]{klingler_if_2003}.
      However, not only were enslaved people from other parts of the US brought to plantations in Louisiana but the \foreign{Américains} themselves moved there to run plantations so that by 1860 in Pointe Coupee, for instance, they were in control of 76\% of the large plantations \parencite[Costello, 1999, as cited in][p.~107]{klingler_if_2003}.
      From these horrific conditions, enslaved people and their descendants developed their own French-based language variety out of whatever means they had at their disposal.
      That effort and their plight is worthy of being remembered every time a word in Creole is spoken today.
    
  \section{Summary}
    Wherein the objective of the present study is to examine the subject pronoun systems of Creoles and Cajuns and how these relate to speakers' identities, several topics warranted overarching reviews.
    To contextualize this study relative to the literature, race and ethnicity in sociolinguistics were discussed.
    Sociolinguistics has always dealt with race and ethnicity and has recently begun reconsidering how race in particular is conceptualized through the raciolinguistics paradigm.
    It is hoped that this study will add to that trajectory in at least some small way.

    The particular groups of interest here are Creoles and Cajuns.
    The typical Cajun origin story has them descending from the Acadians of the Maritime Provinces of Canada, but this criteria for modern-day identifications is rather flexible as long as one can pass as White.
    The Creole social category, on the other hand, began as being very wide open, being essentially a synonym for local or native-born.
    It has morphed several times due to changing social conditions to where it is now largely a sort of local form of Black, although unlike Whiteness for Cajuns, Blackness for Creoles is not a hard requirement.

    Both groups -- all of South Louisiana in truth -- have historically had F\&C as their dominant language.
    While F\&C has had greater staying power in the rural areas of South Louisiana in the 20th century than in metropolitan areas, it has still been consistently in decline as the state has become less and less isolated from the rest of the US.
    Today, there are perhaps no more monolingual speakers of F\&C, and so the language has more symbolic value than practical value.
    However, that symbolic value is heavily in favor of French rather than Creole, the latter having been all but absent in the public domain.
    This absence is in line with a social hierarchy that would place Cajuns above Creoles with the result that all things culturally South Louisiana are branded as Cajun, erasing the role of Creoles.
    As will be seen in the following chapters, these two groups largely produce different subject pronouns, although there are exceptions.
    This overall tendency is not invisible to the community, and how they navigate these associations and personal and group identities will become clearer in the succeeding chapters.